{#-
  The shell every page shares.

  `root` is a relative prefix ("", "../", "../../../") computed per page. Every
  link and asset in this archive is relative, which is what lets the same built
  folder work at a domain root, in a subdirectory, on a USB stick and inside a
  zip a reader downloaded. Absolute paths would quietly break three of those.
-#}
<!doctype html>
{# `lang` is the *interface* language and so is `dir`: the masthead, the
    colophon and every notice on this page are written in it, and it is the
    same on every page of the archive. A page whose document is in another
    language marks that language on the transcription itself (see
    page.html.jinja), which is the only element it applies to.

    This also settles a fight with the search index. Pagefind builds one index
    per `<html lang>` it meets and a client only ever loads the one matching
    the page it is on, so a per-page language here fragments the index unless
    `--force-language` is set - which build/search.py does set, and now agrees
    with what is written here rather than overriding it. -#}
<html lang="{{ ui_lang }}" dir="{{ ui_dir }}">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>{% block title %}{{ collection.title }}{% endblock %}</title>
<meta name="description" content="{{ (page_description or collection.description)|striptags|truncate(180) }}">
<meta name="generator" content="Stackroom {{ collection.build.version }}">
{#- No third-party origin appears anywhere in this document, so the policy can
    be absolute. GitHub Pages cannot set headers, hence the meta tag. -#}
{#- Scripts are strict: same origin only, plus the WebAssembly and worker the
    search index needs. Styles allow inline, because a redaction box's position
    on a scan and a page's aspect ratio are per-element numbers and there is
    nowhere else to put them. That is a real weakening and worth being honest
    about - but with default-src 'none' and img-src limited to this origin,
    there is no host an injected style could reach to send anything to. -#}
<meta http-equiv="Content-Security-Policy" content="default-src 'none'; img-src 'self' data:; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; script-src 'self' 'wasm-unsafe-eval'; worker-src 'self' blob:; font-src 'self'; connect-src 'self'; base-uri 'none'; form-action 'none'">
<link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ root }}assets/stackroom.css">
{#- The scripts that block the parser, and there is exactly one reason a script
    is allowed to: something it does has to have happened before the browser's
    first rendering opportunity. A theme the reader chose has to be on <html>
    before the first paint or they get a flash of the other one on every page;
    a page turn's direction has to be set from `pagereveal`, which fires ahead
    of every deferred script.

    Which scripts, and on which templates, is `HEAD_SCRIPTS` in build/site.py -
    a script opts in there and this file does not change. They are requested
    after the stylesheet the paint was already waiting for, so they arrive
    inside a wait the reader was doing anyway, and they are skipped by the
    deferred sweep at the end of the body, so each is still fetched once. -#}
{#- The interface's own strings, before prefs.js and therefore before every
    other script on the page: prefs.js reads this file once and republishes
    t(), n() and pct() on `window.stackroomReader`, and by the time any
    deferred script runs the sentences are already written in the archive's
    language. Not in HEAD_SCRIPTS because it is generated by the build rather
    than copied out of assets/js, and it has to come first whatever that list
    holds. -#}
<script src="{{ root }}assets/i18n.js"></script>
{%- for script in head_scripts %}
<script src="{{ root }}assets/js/{{ script }}"></script>
{%- endfor %}
{%- if favicon %}
<link rel="icon" href="{{ root }}assets/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml">
{%- endif %}
{%- block head %}{% endblock %}
</head>
<body{% if body_class %} class="{{ body_class }}"{% endif %}>
<a class="skip" href="#main">{{ t('nav.skip') }}</a>

<header class="masthead">
  <div class="wrap {{ wrap }} masthead__inner">
    <p class="masthead__title"><a href="{{ root }}index.html">{{ collection.title }}</a></p>
    <nav class="masthead__nav" aria-label="{{ t('nav.sections') }}">
      <a href="{{ root }}browse/index.html"{% if nav == 'browse' %} aria-current="page"{% endif %}>{{ t('nav.documents') }}</a>
      {%- if search_enabled %}
      <a href="{{ root }}search/index.html"{% if nav == 'search' %} aria-current="page"{% endif %}>{{ t('nav.search') }}</a>
      {%- endif %}
      <a href="{{ root }}withheld/index.html"{% if nav == 'withheld' %} aria-current="page"{% endif %}>{{ t('nav.withheld') }}</a>
      {#- Only on a build that has a comparison: `compare_enabled` is set by
          `compare.build()`, which SiteBuilder.run() calls unconditionally, so
          the global is always defined and is false on an ordinary build.

          `nav.compare` is in all four catalogues, and so is everything behind
          this link: compare.html.jinja and compare_index.html.jinja carry a
          `t()` call for every string and `compare.*` is the largest group in
          en.json. This label was the last English word left on a translated
          comparison page. -#}
      {%- if compare_enabled %}
      <a href="{{ root }}compare/index.html"{% if nav == 'compare' %} aria-current="page"{% endif %}>{{ t('nav.compare') }}</a>
      {%- endif %}
      <a href="{{ root }}about/index.html"{% if nav == 'about' %} aria-current="page"{% endif %}>{{ t('nav.about') }}</a>
      {#- A real link, so it works with no script at all; the stylesheet keeps
          it out of the way until the reader is two screens down, which needs
          no scroll listener and no JavaScript. -#}
      <a class="to-top" href="#main"><span aria-hidden="true">↑</span> {{ t('nav.top') }}</a>
    </nav>
  </div>
</header>

<main id="main" tabindex="-1">
{%- block main %}{% endblock %}
</main>

<footer class="colophon">
  <div class="wrap {{ wrap }}">
    {#- An archive earns trust by being checkable. This block is the cheapest
        possible way to say: here is exactly what was built, from exactly which
        bytes, by exactly which version, and you can verify all three. -#}
    {#- Two counts in one sentence, so the counts are pluralised on their own
        and the sentence takes them as finished noun phrases. Assembling it the
        other way - a stem plus an 's' - is untranslatable into any language
        with more than two plural forms, which is most of them. -#}
    <p>{{ t('footer.built',
            documents=t('count.documents', count=collection.stats.documents),
            pages=t('count.pages', count=collection.stats.pages),
            date=dt(collection.build.built_at),
            version=collection.build.version) }}</p>
    <p>{{ t('footer.digest_html', digest=collection.build.source_digest[:16]) }} ·
       <a href="{{ root }}manifest.json">{{ t('footer.manifest') }}</a></p>
    {%- if collection.license %}
    <p>{{ collection.license }}</p>
    {%- endif %}
  </div>
</footer>
{#- Every file in assets/js is loaded on every page, deferred, except the ones
    this page already took in the head. They are small, independent
    enhancements; each one checks whether the thing it enhances is on this page
    and does nothing if it is not. -#}
{%- for script in extra_scripts if script not in head_scripts %}
<script src="{{ root }}assets/js/{{ script }}" defer></script>
{%- endfor %}
{%- block scripts %}{% endblock %}
</body>
</html>
